course: dictionary
lesson: 7647
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one the writer means. Project is a good example. As a noun, with the stress at the start (PROJ-ect), it is a piece of planned work or a big plan. As a verb, with the stress at the end (pro-JECT), it is an action, such as shining a picture onto a wall.
explain: «Cheating in a group project can hurt the whole team.» Group and team are the clues. A project done by a group is a piece of planned work, so that is the meaning here. Try your meaning in the sentence: a planned piece of work fits, so it is right.
explain: «Actors project their voices» tells you the verb. Actors do not shine their voices onto a wall. Actors speak loudly and clearly so people far away can hear. Always test each meaning in the sentence and keep the one that makes sense.
fact: As a noun, a project is a piece of planned work, often done over a period of time, or a big plan, like building a bridge.
fact: As a verb, to project means to shine a picture onto a screen or wall, to stick out beyond an edge, to speak loudly so people far away hear, or to guess a future number from what is known now.
fact: Projected numbers are estimates, which means careful guesses based on what is known now.
fact: Noun: PROJ-ect. Verb: pro-JECT. The stress moves to a different syllable.
exercise: «For a project, you find facts with the library catalogue and the index.» What is a project here? | *A piece of planned school work | A machine that shines pictures | To stick out beyond an edge | A shelf in the library
exercise: «Actors project their voices so people far away can hear.» What does project mean here? | To guess a future number | To stick out beyond an edge | *To speak loudly and clearly | To shine a picture onto a wall
exercise: «The projected numbers for next year are estimates.» What does projected mean? | *Guessed from what is known now | Cut short | Planned to be built | Shone onto a screen
test: «The class worked on a group project about volcanoes.» Which word tells you the project was done by several people? | about | volcanoes | *group | class
test: «The engineers had a project to build a bridge over the river.» Which meaning of project fits best? | *A big plan | To stick out beyond an edge | To shine a picture onto a screen | To speak loudly
exam: Pupil says project is always a noun. Is that right? | No, it is always a verb and never a thing | *No, it can also be a verb, so check the sentence | Yes, but only in science lessons | Yes, it only ever names a thing
